title: Why a run commits each link on its own
tags: Sync
kind: rationale
audience: dev
summary: A run commits each link as soon as it is synced, not by batches: what a stop makes the next plan redo is a comparison, never a transfer, and batching saved one write per link among several — batches removed in November 2026.

A run takes the links one at a time, parents first: the link's metadata, then its
files, then its `POST …/links/commit` ([[Running a sync]]). A link stopped half-way
is left uncommitted; every link before it is committed.

!! What batches were for

Until November 2026 a run grouped its links in batches — at most
`commit-batch-size` links (50), fewer once they held `transfer-batch-size` copies
(20), both set in the intents file's `[settings]` — and committed each batch at once.
The idea was to trade the cost of a commit against the work a crash makes the next
run redo, a transfer being the slow part.

Neither half held:

* ''A transfer is never redone''. A successful operation leaves the plan at once,
  whatever its batch; what an uncommitted link costs the next `mf sync plan` is to
  see both sides' stamps moved and compare the two files' bytes — equal, nothing to
  copy ([[Change detection in sync]]). A batch only made more links pay that reading.
* ''A commit is one write among several''. Each operation already writes the plan
  repository to leave it, and each sync writes the records it changes: one commit more per link
  does not change the order of a run's writes.

So a run commits each link on its own, and an intents file that still has
`[settings]` is refused, naming the keys to remove ([[The intents file]]). The
`links/commit` endpoint still takes a list ([[Sync endpoints]]).
